<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 10px; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> My Evening Routine</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I'm Sleepy. It's Bedtime.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Summarize a text using a graphic organizer. Reading: Outline the meaning of simple paragraphs. Phonemic Awareness: Blend spoken phonemes to form one-syllable or two-syllable words if these words have been previously encountered.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can summarize bedtime routines in a graphic organizer (e.g., "First, I put on my pajamas. Then, I brush my teeth. Last, I go to bed."). Can outline main ideas of bedtime stories with simple keywords (e.g., "sleep," "brush," and "night"). Can blend spoken phonemes to form one-syllable or two-syllable words related to daily routines (e.g., /wɔːʃ/ in the word "wash" and /t-iːθ/ in the word "teeth").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 52%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification. Activate prior knowledge related to "My Evening Routine" and "I'm Sleepy. It's Bedtime."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows flashcards of evening routine actions (e.g., brushing teeth, washing face, putting on pajamas, reading a book). Students call out the action or word (e.g., "brush teeth!"). Teacher asks, "What do you do before bed?" eliciting short answers like "I brush teeth." or "I read a book."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of key vocabulary from Lesson 1, specifically "bed," "teeth," "wash," "brush," "pajamas," "read," and "sleep." The teacher clearly pronounces /b/ in "bed" and /t/ in "teeth." The teacher also models phonemic blending by slowly pronouncing /w-ɒ-ʃ/ then blending to "wash," and /t-iː-θ/ then blending to "teeth," asking students to repeat. The teacher says simple sentences: "First, I wash my face. Then, I brush my teeth."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher uses visuals (flashcards) and gestures to check understanding.
                    <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do I brush my hair or my teeth?" (Teeth!)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do I wash my face with water or juice?" (Water!)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content related to "My Evening Routine." Introduce a short, simple text for reading comprehension.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, illustrated narrative text titled "Mia's Bedtime" (printed on a large chart or projected). The text will describe a simple evening routine using sequencing words (First, then, last), present simple, and target vocabulary (bed, pajamas, teeth, brush, wash, story, book, sleepy, night).
                    <br>Example Text:
                    <br><i>"It is night. Mia is sleepy.
                    <br>First, Mia puts on her soft pajamas.
                    <br>Then, she goes to the bathroom. She washes her face with water. She takes her toothbrush and brushes her teeth.
                    <br>Next, Mia's mom reads her a story from a big book.
                    <br>Last, Mia goes to her comfortable bed. She pulls up her blanket and closes her eyes. "Goodnight, Mom! Goodnight!" Mia says. She dreams happy dreams."</i></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Identifies the main idea and supporting details in short, simple written texts about familiar topics.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> The teacher reads the text aloud once, pointing to words. Then asks, "What is the story about? Morning or night?" (Night.) "Who is the story about?" (Mia.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Activity:</b> The teacher reads simple statements. Students show 'thumbs up' for True and 'thumbs down' for False.
                            <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin: 0;">
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Mia reads a book." (False - Mom reads.)</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Mia brushes her teeth." (True.)</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Mia goes to bed." (True.)</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Matching Activity:</b> Students receive a worksheet with three pictures depicting actions from the story (e.g., girl brushing teeth, girl putting on pajamas, mom reading a book). Students draw a line from each picture to the sentence in the text that describes it.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading skills, specifically outlining meaning and sequencing events from the text.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading comprehension, identifying sequence (First, then, next, last), present simple for routines, vocabulary (pajamas, wash, teeth, brush, story, book, bed, night, sleepy).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Cloze Reading):</b> Students receive a worksheet with sentences from Mia's routine. Some key words (verbs or sequencing words) are missing. Students read the sentences and choose the correct word from a word bank to complete them.
                            <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin: 0;">
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "___, Mia puts on her pajamas." (First)</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "She ___ her teeth." (brushes)</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled (Sentence Ordering & Outlining):</b> The teacher provides strips of paper with jumbled sentences from the story (e.g., "She brushes her teeth.", "Mia puts on her pajamas.", "Mom reads a story."). In small groups, students read and arrange the sentences in the correct order of Mia's routine. After ordering, they identify one keyword for each sentence (e.g., "pajamas," "brush," "story") and write it next to the ordered sentence, outlining the main idea of each step.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Reading & Peer Check:</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a short paragraph from the "Mia's Bedtime" story (e.g., the paragraph about brushing teeth). They take turns reading it aloud to their partner, focusing on pronunciation, especially /b/ and /t/, and blending words like "wash" and "teeth." After reading, they ask each other "What does Mia do here?" and use keywords to answer. (e.g., "She washes her face and brushes her teeth.").</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language to summarize a routine, focusing on reading comprehension output.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Individual activity. Students receive a blank "My Evening Routine" graphic organizer with three boxes labeled "First," "Then," and "Last." They reread the "Mia's Bedtime" story silently. After reading, they use the graphic organizer to summarize Mia's evening routine by writing a simple sentence or drawing a picture and writing keywords in each box, connecting directly to the specific objective of summarizing a text using a graphic organizer.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme/Scenario:</b> The task directly applies the "My Evening Routine" scenario to demonstrate comprehension of "I'm Sleepy. It's Bedtime."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Reading comprehension, ability to extract main ideas, use of sequencing words (First, Then, Last), and accurate use of target vocabulary (pajamas, brush teeth, read story, go to bed).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">7-8 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment of reading comprehension and summarizing skills.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist while students are completing the graphic organizer, and later, review of the completed graphic organizers. The teacher assesses the clarity and accuracy of the summary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their completed "My Evening Routine" graphic organizers.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Summarizes the main steps of Mia's evening routine accurately (3 points).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses at least two sequencing words (First, Then, Last) correctly (2 points).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Includes at least 3-4 target vocabulary words (e.g., pajamas, brush teeth, story, bed) (2 points).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Outlines main ideas of the paragraphs with simple keywords (e.g., "pajamas," "brush," "story," "bed") (2 points).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Overall clarity and legibility (1 point).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:
                    <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can read a story about an evening routine. (Circle: Yes / A little / No)"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can put the routine in order. (Circle: Yes / A little / No)"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Reflect on the lesson's effectiveness and plan for future adjustments and progression.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did you learn about reading stories about evening routines today?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of the reading activities was easy? What was difficult?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b> The reading lesson went well, with most students able to follow the story and identify key events. The graphic organizer effectively helped students summarize the text. Some students needed more guidance with outlining main ideas using only keywords, often wanting to write full sentences. The phonemic blending for "wash" and "teeth" was engaging. For the next lesson (Speaking), I will ensure students have opportunities to orally recount their own or Mia's routine, using the sequencing words and target vocabulary. I will also incorporate polite expressions like "Goodnight, sleep well!" and "Can you read me a story, please?" into speaking practice.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Next Lesson:</b> This reading lesson provided a strong foundation for understanding a sequenced routine. The next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction) will leverage this comprehension by having students orally describe evening routines, either Mia's or their own, using the target vocabulary and grammatical structures practiced today. This will move them from receptive (reading) to productive (speaking) use of the language.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>